WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:09.000
The average Canadian home price exceeds six hundred and eighty thousand dollars in 2024 — up from approximately two hundred and fifty thousand in 2005.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.170
The price-to-income ratio has climbed from four to one to ten to one in under two decades.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:20.431
Homeownership among Canadians under thirty-five has dropped thirty-six percent since the 2006 Census.

00:00:20.431 --> 00:00:26.272
The national rental vacancy rate sits at one point five percent — critically low by any measure.

00:00:26.272 --> 00:00:30.238
Median household income is approximately seventy-five thousand dollars.

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:37.545
The National Housing Strategy committed eighty-nine billion dollars in 2017 and has failed to meet its own delivery targets.

00:00:37.545 --> 00:00:42.431
Under the Veterans Land Act, a home was purchasable for less than two years' wages.

00:00:42.431 --> 00:00:44.999
Today that number exceeds nine years.

00:00:44.999 --> 00:00:46.761
This is not a market failure.

00:00:46.761 --> 00:00:49.818
This is policy failure compounded over decades.
